# Cross-agent handoffs (the shared inbox)

One place where a change in **one** domain that affects **other** domains is announced — so the other
agents learn it by *pull* (reading), not by being told in chat. Skim it at the start of every session
(it is in the universal onboarding protocol). Curated by the steward; **substance is written by the
domain agent** (the steward routes and keeps lifecycle, it does not author).

> ⚠️ **The entry grammar below is LOAD-BEARING.** Three deterministic parsers read this file
> (`/crit-status-check`'s owed-counter, `/crit-doc-lint [5]` inbox hygiene, `/crit-doc-lint [7]` token vocabulary).
> An entry that deviates is not "slightly off" — it is **invisible to the counter that tells an agent
> what it owes**. Keep the shape exactly.

## Entry grammar

```
- YYYY-MM-DD — AGENT <N> → AGENT <M> (+AGENT <K> FYI): **[<TOKEN> — … Done when: <observable>]** <what changed → what the recipient must do>. [`ref`]
```

Each part earns its place:

| Part | Rule | Why (what breaks otherwise) |
|---|---|---|
| `- YYYY-MM-DD ` | line starts with `- `, then an absolute date | the parsers split entries on this; no date = no entry |
| ` — ` | em-dash between date and participants | delimiter |
| `AGENT N → AGENT M` | real arrow `→` (U+2192), **not** `->` | the counter splits on it to find sender/recipient |
| `(+AGENT K FYI)` | optional; extra readers only | **one owner per entry** — shared responsibility is held by nobody |
| `:` | one colon closing the participants | the token is read at the FIRST colon; a colon *before* the arrow breaks the parse |
| `**[TOKEN …]**` | bolded, immediately after the colon | the token is read at its syntactic *position*, never searched for |
| `Done when: <observable>` | mandatory on `[OPEN]` | without it the recipient cannot know when to flip the token → stale `[OPEN]` |
| the body | ONE line (the file is line-oriented) | a multi-line entry is truncated at the first newline |

## Lifecycle tokens — a CLOSED vocabulary

Live (the recipient still owes something):

- `[OPEN — … Done when: <observable>]` — awaiting the recipient's action/ACK.
- `[PARKED-OWNED <date> by A<n> — … Park-trigger: <what un-parks it>]` — owned but deliberately
  deferred; stays visible until its owner closes it.

Settled (a record; may be rotated to the archive):
`[ABSORBED <date> by A<n> — …]` · `[ACK — …]` · `[CLOSED — …]` · `[SUPERSEDED by <ref>]` ·
`[RETRACTED — …]`. A pure FYI (no action for anyone) needs no token.

**An invented token is invisible.** The owed-counter recognizes only `OPEN` and `PARKED-OWNED` as
live and treats **everything else as settled** — so an ask under a made-up token
(`[GREEN-LIT …]`, `[CONFIRMED …]`, `[URGENT …]`) is *owed by someone and counted by nobody*, and the
next rotation buries it unread. `/crit-doc-lint [7]` flags any token outside this list.

## Rules the linter enforces (`/crit-doc-lint [5]`)

- **`Done when:` on every `[OPEN]`** — the measured cause of the stale-token class.
- **One owner per entry** — address exactly one agent; the rest are `(+… FYI)`.
- **PING-PONG detection** — a topic with ≥3 hops and ≥2 reversals is flagged. That signals an unclear
  boundary or an undecided question: **another handoff will not settle it.** Name the owner, or
  escalate to the user.

## How to read it

**Top-down, newest-first, IN FULL — never `grep`-snippeted.** A search renders long single-line
entries as `[Omitted long matching line]`, silently hiding exactly the entries you must act on.
Address the newest `[OPEN]` entries addressed to you **before** re-touching older ones.

**Absorb-or-ACK.** When you act on an entry addressed to you: either land the work **and** post a
one-line ACK at the top, or flip its token to `[ABSORBED <date> by A<n> — <what you did>]` in place.

**Not a backlog, not a changelog.** Only what another agent must *act on or not undo*. Durable
rationale goes in the relevant `DECISIONS.md`; this file is the short pointer.

**A handoff is a request for ACTION, never a receipt.** If you only changed your own domain's logic
inside a file another agent owns, **the commit is the record** — the owner does not revert an
in-domain edit, so you owe no entry. (This single rule removes most inbox traffic.) Who holds the
pen on each write surface is named in the roster.

## Rotation

The steward moves settled traffic to a dated archive under `archive/` so the live inbox stays
readable. **Two classes never rotate:** live tokens (`OPEN` / `PARKED-OWNED`), and any entry whose
token is **not in the vocabulary above** — an unrecognized value must be found late, never lost.
**Rotating REBASES relative links** (the archive sits one directory deeper): re-run `/crit-doc-lint [1]`
after every rotation and read its output.

**Retiring the whole channel** (the inbox itself stops existing as a channel — e.g. its actor left,
or the register moved): do **not** delete the file — a missing file cannot warn anyone, and the
channel gets resurrected by the next writer. Mark the **first line** with `⚰ RETIRED <date>` and
state where the live register is. The controls read the marker: the owed-counter prints *"not a
channel"* instead of a clean zero, and the layout detector stops counting the file as evidence of
an agentic layer.

